// A handle (a stream, a listener, a process, a watcher) is equal only to
// itself, and handles order and hash by identity: an order, a sort or a
// map keyed by handles is stable within a run, not between runs.
mod io;
mod fs;
mod process;
mod tcp;
mod tls;
mod time;
mod net;
mod dirs;

/// the command line arguments. argv[0] names the program: the script
/// file, or a standalone binary's own path. A program an embedder runs
/// from source gets the arguments the embedder passes.
val args: fn() -> Array<string>;

/// join parts to path using the OS specific path separator
val join_path: fn(path: string, @args: [string, Array<string>]) -> string;

/// flush stdout/stderr and end the program with the given exit code; a
/// code outside 0..=255 exits with 1. The cycle in which the call fires
/// is the program's last. It runs to its end, so what else it prints is
/// printed and every call it makes is made, and nothing runs after it:
/// a `<-` it queued never lands, a reply still on its way is never
/// delivered, and an async call still at work (a file write, a send)
/// may be cut short. Exit on the event that says the work is done. The
/// return type is fully polymorphic because the call never
/// actually returns; it fires when its argument produces a value, so
/// you can use it as the trailing expression of a program to make it
/// exit deterministically on completion.
val exit: fn(code: i64) -> 'a;
